---
// The styles of the game belong to the page: the picture of the game that the page renders on the
// server and the game itself use the same ones, which are sent once
import './components/word-radar.css';

import { type GameDefinition } from '@/games/registry';
import GameLayout from '@/layouts/GameLayout.astro';

import WordRadar from './components/WordRadar';
import WordRadarSkeleton from './components/WordRadarSkeleton';

interface Props {
  game: GameDefinition;
}

const { game } = Astro.props;
---

<GameLayout game={game}>
  {/* Starts fetching the words (about 2 MB) while the game itself is still loading */}
  <script>
    import { loadWordData } from './data/load';

    loadWordData().catch(() => {
      // The game shows the error itself
    });
  </script>
  {/* Rendered in the browser only: the hidden word is random */}
  <WordRadar client:only="react">
    <WordRadarSkeleton slot="fallback" />
  </WordRadar>
  <noscript>
    <p class="text-muted">
      This game needs JavaScript to run. Nothing is sent anywhere, it all happens in your browser.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div slot="rules">
    <p>
      A word is hidden. Type any word you like: the game tells you how <strong>close</strong> it is
      to the hidden word in <strong>meaning</strong>, as a <strong>rank</strong>. Rank 1 is the
      hidden word itself, rank 2 is the word that is nearest to it, and so on out of about 30,000
      common English words. A lower rank is better.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The radar</strong> shows your guesses as dots. The hidden word is in the middle, the
        closer a guess is in meaning the closer its dot is to the middle. The rings mark rank 10,
        100, 1,000 and 10,000.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The list</strong> sorts your guesses by rank, with a bar and a word for how close
        they are: Burning (top 10), Hot (top 100), Warm (top 1,000), Cool (top 5,000) and Cold.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Hint</strong> gives you a word that is closer than your best guess.{' '}
        <strong>Give up</strong> shows the hidden word.
      </li>
      <li>
        When the round is over, the <strong>map</strong> shows where your guesses were compared to
        the hidden word, and the list shows the words that were nearest to it.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Think in meanings, not in letters: <em>wolf</em> is much closer to <em>dog</em> than{' '}
      <em>dot</em> is. Only words from the word list work; if a word is refused, try the singular or
      another form. Rounds are unlimited and nothing is saved. The link of a round opens the same
      hidden word for whoever you send it to.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="keys">
    <ul>
      <li>
        <kbd>Enter</kbd> hands in the word you typed
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Esc</kbd> empties the field
      </li>
      <li>
        <kbd>Tab</kbd> moves between the field, the buttons and the lists
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The radar is a picture of the list: everything it shows is also in the list, which screen
      readers can read. Each guess is announced with its rank and how close it is.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div slot="built">
    <p>
      How can a computer know that <em>wolf</em> is close to <em>dog</em>? It reads numbers. The
      game uses <strong>GloVe</strong> word vectors from Stanford University: every word is a list
      of 100 numbers that was learned from billions of words of text, so that words that appear in
      similar places get similar numbers. The closeness of two words is the{' '}
      <strong>cosine similarity</strong> of their numbers (the angle between the two lists, seen as
      arrows).
    </p>
    <p>
      The numbers were prepared once, offline, with a script that is part of the repository: the
      30,000 commonest words that are ordinary English words in <strong>WordNet</strong> (this
      leaves out names and little words such as <em>the</em>), shrunk from 100 to 64 numbers with a{' '}
      <strong>principal component analysis</strong> (it keeps the directions in which the words
      differ most, 82.5 percent of the variation), and stored as whole numbers from -127 to 127.
      That makes about 2 MB. The hidden words are everyday things from WordNet (animals, objects,
      food, plants, parts of the body, materials), checked by a list of words that must not be
      hidden.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the start of a round the browser compares the hidden word with all 30,000 words, sorts them
      (a few milliseconds) and so knows the rank of every possible guess. The direction of a dot
      around the radar comes from how the word differs from the hidden word, seen on a plane that is
      found from the 300 words that are nearest to the hidden word, so that words that are related
      to it in similar ways tend to lie near each other. The map after the round uses a principal
      component analysis of the words of this round, calculated in the browser. The logic is small
      pure TypeScript functions with unit tests; the data has tests, too (for example that{' '}
      <em>king</em> is closer to <em>queen</em> than to <em>banana</em>). Nothing is sent anywhere
      and nothing is stored on your device.
    </p>
  </div>
</GameLayout>
